 can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**Alright, let\'s break this down.** The current situation is pretty straightforward. I\'m a Peasant (teammate on the left, Landlord on the right), and the Landlord just played a King of Clubs. My teammate countered with an Ace of Diamonds.  I need to beat that Ace.\n\nMy hand is *loaded*. I\'ve got a killer straight from 3 to Ace, a pair of Tens, a lone Three, and *the* ultimate weapon: the Rocket (Black and Red Jokers).  Playing either Joker would beat the Ace, but at what cost?  It would break my Rocket!  Or, I can use the Rocket and beat it. Technically. But, I am thinking, what if I keep my rocket and straight combo intact?\n\nHere\'s the thing.  My teammate played the Ace.  That\'s a strong move, probably designed to force the Landlord to play a Two or pass.  I *could* break my Rocket and win this trick, but that\'s just a waste of a massive bomb.  My teammate is likely trying to drain the Landlord\'s resources.  I don\'t need to try and control the table here.\n\nThe Landlord has 19 cards, which tells me the game is still early.  If I play a Joker to win the trick, I\'m essentially "capping" my teammate and also potentially wasting the Joker by using it on a single card. I also need my Jokers (if I choose not to use the Rocket) to make sure I can beat other strong combos from the Landlord later.  The Landlord is likely to try to bomb or rocket me, so I need to preserve my Jokers. Or the Landlord will think they have control, and I pull out the rocket.  The 12 card straight is my primary goal, and my secondary goal is the Rocket. If I play the Jokers now, my hand will be weakened and the Landlord can potentially win.\n\nAlso, it doesn\'t list the Twos. Meaning they are all played out.\n\nSo, the smart play here is to *Pass*. Let the Landlord deal with the Ace. If they have a Two, great, that\'s one less high card they have. If they *don\'t* have a Two, my teammate gets control back, which is good for us.  I maintain my Rocket, keep my options open for a massive straight, and don\'t waste my resources on a single card that my teammate has already played. No need to throw away my rocket to beat a single A!\n\nMy goal is the long straight and the Rocket. No need to rush now.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My hand is exceptionally strong, containing a Rocket (Black Joker + Red Joker) and a potential 12-card straight (3 through A). My teammate has just played an Ace to beat the Landlord\'s King. This is a strong control move. Beating the Ace would require me to use a Joker, which would break my Rocket and waste a top-tier card on a teammate\'s trick. The optimal strategy is to allow the Ace to pressure the Landlord. The Landlord must now either spend a 2/Joker or allow my teammate to maintain control. Preserving the Rocket ensures I can regain initiative later to play my long straight or stop the Landlord.\n\nReason: Beating a teammate\'s Ace with a Joker is counter-productive as it wastes the highest cards on a friendly target and breaks the Rocket, which is my hand\'s primary winning condition and defense. Passing forces the Landlord to respond to the high card.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
